            'excerpt' => 'Learn how a laser marking machine adds permanent serial numbers, QR and Data Matrix codes to metal and plastic parts, and which xTool suits small batches.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'Laser marking puts a serial number, a QR or Data Matrix code, a logo or nameplate data permanently onto a part, tool or package – with no ink or labels to rub off or peel away. For metal, choose a fibre or MOPA laser (in the xTool range, the F2 Ultra with a 60 W MOPA laser or the F1 Ultra with a 20 W fibre laser); for heat-sensitive plastics, choose a UV laser – the F2 Ultra UV. For small-batch production, a desktop laser marking machine like these is enough, but it does not replace an automated industrial line.' ],
                [ 'p', 'This guide is for workshops and small manufacturers laser marking serial numbers and QR codes on parts, tools, housings, packaging or promotional items. We cover engraving gifts and jewellery and colours on steel in a separate article on metal engraving.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Marking, engraving or deep engraving?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Each method trades durability against time:' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Marking: the laser changes the colour or structure of the surface and removes almost no material (on stainless steel this is annealing – a dark mark under a smooth surface). It is the fastest method and usually enough for codes.',
                    'Engraving: the laser vaporises a thin layer of material. You can feel the mark, and it stands up better to abrasion.',
                    'Deep engraving: many passes remove material layer by layer. It suits marks exposed to heavy wear, but every tenth of a millimetre costs time.',
                ] ],
                [ 'h3', 'How common materials react' ],
