                    'Stainless steel: annealing gives a dark mark with no groove. It does change the oxide layer, though, so for sterilised or corrosion-exposed parts, check the customer’s requirements.',
                    'Anodised aluminium: the laser bleaches the dyed anodic layer, so the code comes out light on a dark background.',
                    'Bare aluminium, carbon steel, brass: the laser engraves or darkens the surface; on bare aluminium, contrast is easier to achieve with a MOPA laser.',
                    'Plastics: infrared lasers (fibre lasers work at around 1064 nm) darken light plastics or foam dark ones, depending on the polymer and additives. A UV laser marks many plastics with minimal heating.',
                    'Painted and powder-coated metal: the laser strips the coating.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'The rule for production: choose the shallowest method that gives contrast and lasts the part’s whole life, and test on the same material before a batch.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'What data and codes can you laser mark?' ],
                [ 'p', 'In small-batch production, a laser usually marks:' ],
