                    'Logos, the manufacturer’s name and conformity marks',
                    'Nameplate data: model, ratings, warnings',
                    'Scales and asset numbers on tools',
                ] ],
                [ 'h3', 'Why permanent marking matters' ],
                [ 'p', 'A label can peel off, but a laser mark is part of the surface. It gives you traceability: a batch number links a part to its production date, material and order, which makes complaints and recalls of a faulty batch easier. It makes counterfeiting harder, because a unique code is hard to remove without a trace and, once scanned, can lead to a verification page. And a logo on the product itself outlasts a sticker. In regulated industries such as medical devices, automotive or aerospace, standards and the customer define the content and method of marking.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'QR and Data Matrix codes: what to keep in mind' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'A customer’s phone reads a QR code, so it suits packaging and promotional items; the compact Data Matrix is the usual code for direct part marking.',
                    'Leave a blank margin around the code and don’t shrink it too far: each module must be clearly wider than the laser line.',
                    'Inverted codes on anodised aluminium and glare on shiny steel make reading harder – test with the customer’s scanner.',
                    'Error correction is a reserve for wear, not for weak marking. Grading code quality to a standard takes a separate verifier, not the laser.',
